🔗 Merge Two Arrays
Definition

Merging two arrays means combining the elements of two arrays into a single array.

Example
Array 1: [10, 20, 30]
Array 2: [40, 50, 60]


Merged:  [10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]
Method 1: Using + Operator
arr1 = [10, 20, 30]
arr2 = [40, 50, 60]


merged_arr = arr1 + arr2


print(merged_arr)
Output
[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]
Method 2: Using extend()
arr1 = [10, 20, 30]
arr2 = [40, 50, 60]


arr1.extend(arr2)


print(arr1)
Output
[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]

extend() modifies the original arr1.

Method 3: Using a Loop

This method helps us understand how merging works internally.

arr1 = [10, 20, 30]
arr2 = [40, 50, 60]


merged_arr = []


for item in arr1:
    merged_arr.append(item)


for item in arr2:
    merged_arr.append(item)


print(merged_arr)
Output
[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]
Merging Arrays With Different Elements

The arrays do not need to have the same size.

arr1 = [1, 2]
arr2 = [3, 4, 5, 6]


merged_arr = arr1 + arr2


print(merged_arr)
Output
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
Merging Two Sorted Arrays

Suppose:

arr1 = [1, 3, 5]
arr2 = [2, 4, 6]

Simply combining them gives:

[1, 3, 5, 2, 4, 6]

This is not sorted.

To merge them while keeping the result sorted, we compare elements from both arrays.

Using Two Pointers
def merge_sorted_arrays(arr1, arr2):
    i = 0
    j = 0
    merged = []


    while i < len(arr1) and j < len(arr2):


        if arr1[i] < arr2[j]:
            merged.append(arr1[i])
            i += 1
        else:
            merged.append(arr2[j])
            j += 1


    while i < len(arr1):
        merged.append(arr1[i])
        i += 1


    while j < len(arr2):
        merged.append(arr2[j])
        j += 1


    return merged




arr1 = [1, 3, 5]
arr2 = [2, 4, 6]


print(merge_sorted_arrays(arr1, arr2))
Output
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
How the Two-Pointer Method Works
arr1 = [1, 3, 5]
         ↑


arr2 = [2, 4, 6]
         ↑

Compare 1 and 2.

1 is smaller, so add it to the merged array.

merged = [1]

Move the first pointer:

arr1 = [1, 3, 5]
            ↑


arr2 = [2, 4, 6]
         ↑

Compare 3 and 2.

2 is smaller:

merged = [1, 2]

Continue until both arrays are processed.

Final result:

[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
Time and Space Complexity

Assume:

First array has n elements.
Second array has m elements.
Method	Time Complexity	Extra Space
+ operator	O(n + m)	O(n + m)
extend()	O(m) additional work	O(1) extra space*
Loop	O(n + m)	O(n + m)
Two Pointers	O(n + m)	O(n + m)

* Ignoring possible internal resizing of the Python list.

Key Takeaways
Merging combines two arrays into one.
The + operator creates a new array.
extend() adds elements to the existing array.
A loop can be used to merge arrays manually.
Two pointers are useful for merging sorted arrays efficiently.
Merging two sorted arrays takes O(n + m) time.
